INSERT INTO public.qb_questions (subject_id, difficulty, question_type, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, points, tags) VALUES
('8b81a645-306d-4cc1-962a-ab597d640fe5', 'advanced', 'multiple_choice', 'A merchandiser calculates that Style A (GMROI=4.2) appears to outperform Style B (GMROI=2.8), but a deeper analysis reveals Style A''s inventory sits mostly in a HIGH-traffic flagship store location with premium floor space allocated, while Style B is mostly in lower-traffic secondary locations. What CRITICAL confounding factor does the raw GMROI comparison fail to isolate?', '["No confounding factor exists; GMROI comparisons are always automatically valid regardless of where inventory happens to be physically located within the retail network","Store LOCATION QUALITY (traffic, premium floor space allocation) is itself a resource with real opportunity cost — Style A''s superior GMROI may partly reflect the ADVANTAGE of being placed in premium retail space rather than the style''s own INTRINSIC merchandising quality, meaning a fair comparison of the styles'' true individual performance should ideally control for or account for this location-quality difference, similar to the earlier store-performance-diagnostics principle of investigating location-specific factors before concluding a performance gap reflects the compared item''s own inherent merit","Store location has no relationship to GMROI or any inventory performance metric, making this comparison scenario''s premise about a confounding factor technically invalid","GMROI is calculated in a way that already automatically and fully adjusts for store location quality differences, making this concern entirely unnecessary"]'::jsonb, 'Store LOCATION QUALITY (traffic, premium floor space allocation) is itself a resource with real opportunity cost — Style A''s superior GMROI may partly reflect the ADVANTAGE of being placed in premium retail space rather than the style''s own INTRINSIC merchandising quality, meaning a fair comparison of the styles'' true individual performance should ideally control for or account for this location-quality difference, similar to the earlier store-performance-diagnostics principle of investigating location-specific factors before concluding a performance gap reflects the compared item''s own inherent merit', 'This directly extends the earlier store-performance-diagnostics principle to a metric-comparison context — GMROI measures return relative to INVENTORY investment, but doesn''t separately isolate the value contributed by PLACEMENT/location quality (premium floor space and high traffic are themselves valuable, finite resources with real opportunity cost) — a style benefiting from superior placement will show inflated GMROI partly due to that placement advantage, not purely its own intrinsic merchandising appeal, meaning a genuinely fair comparison between two styles'' TRUE relative performance should account for or control for this confounding placement-quality difference, rather than taking the raw GMROI comparison at face value.', 3, '{"GMROI-location-confound"}'::text[]),
('8b81a645-306d-4cc1-962a-ab597d640fe5', 'advanced', 'multiple_choice', 'A merchandiser negotiating a buyer-supplier ''chargeback'' dispute argues that a specific late-delivery chargeback should be WAIVED because the delay was caused by the BUYER''s OWN late fabric-color approval (a buyer-side delay upstream of the factory''s control). What documentation/process discipline (established earlier) is MOST critical for the supplier to successfully defend this argument?', '["No documentation is needed; verbal recollection of what happened is always considered fully sufficient and equally persuasive as written records in any commercial dispute of this kind","Formal, dated written communication records (e.g., documented approval requests, dated email/system timestamps showing exactly when color approval was requested versus when it was actually received) — since proving the delay''s TRUE root cause (buyer-side approval delay, not factory production delay) requires objective, timestamped evidence, directly connecting to the earlier PO-documentation/dispute-prevention principle that informal, undocumented communication creates exactly this kind of unresolvable after-the-fact disagreement","The supplier should simply accept the chargeback without any dispute, since chargebacks are always considered final and non-negotiable regardless of the actual underlying cause","Documentation is only relevant for original purchase order terms, never for actual TIMELINE/approval-related disputes that arise DURING order execution"]'::jsonb, 'Formal, dated written communication records (e.g., documented approval requests, dated email/system timestamps showing exactly when color approval was requested versus when it was actually received) — since proving the delay''s TRUE root cause (buyer-side approval delay, not factory production delay) requires objective, timestamped evidence, directly connecting to the earlier PO-documentation/dispute-prevention principle that informal, undocumented communication creates exactly this kind of unresolvable after-the-fact disagreement', 'This directly extends the earlier PO-documentation/dispute-prevention and TNA-calendar discussions to a real chargeback dispute scenario — successfully defending against an unfair chargeback requires objective, DATED evidence establishing the TRUE timeline and root cause (exactly when color approval was formally requested versus when the buyer actually provided it) — without this documented timestamp trail, the dispute becomes an unresolvable ''he-said-she-said'' disagreement, which is precisely why the earlier-established discipline of formal, written, dated communication (rather than informal verbal or undocumented exchanges) isn''t just good practice for routine order management, but becomes the CRITICAL evidence base when a legitimate timeline dispute like this one actually arises.', 3, '{"chargeback-dispute-documentation"}'::text[]),
('8b81a645-306d-4cc1-962a-ab597d640fe5', 'advanced', 'multiple_choice', 'A merchandiser analyzing a ''test-and-repeat'' buying strategy (ordering a SMALL initial quantity of a new, unproven style, then quickly REORDERING a larger quantity if early sell-through is strong) recognizes this strategy''s success depends critically on TWO specific supply-chain capabilities established earlier. What are they, and why does LACKING either one undermine the entire strategy?', '["Test-and-repeat strategies require no specific supply-chain capabilities beyond a generic purchase order process, making this question''s premise about critical dependencies technically unfounded","FAST, RELIABLE supplier lead time (so the reorder can actually arrive while the demand window is still open, connecting to the earlier replenishment-program lead-time discussion) AND real-time/near-real-time SELL-THROUGH DATA VISIBILITY (so the decision to reorder is triggered quickly enough based on genuine early signal, not delayed reporting) — lacking EITHER capability breaks the strategy: slow lead time means the reorder arrives too late even with perfect early data, while slow/delayed sales data means the reorder decision itself is triggered too late even with fast supplier lead time","Test-and-repeat strategies work equally well regardless of supplier lead time or sales data visibility speed, making neither capability actually critical to the strategy''s success","Only supplier lead time matters for this strategy; sales data visibility speed has no relationship to whether test-and-repeat buying succeeds or fails"]'::jsonb, 'FAST, RELIABLE supplier lead time (so the reorder can actually arrive while the demand window is still open, connecting to the earlier replenishment-program lead-time discussion) AND real-time/near-real-time SELL-THROUGH DATA VISIBILITY (so the decision to reorder is triggered quickly enough based on genuine early signal, not delayed reporting) — lacking EITHER capability breaks the strategy: slow lead time means the reorder arrives too late even with perfect early data, while slow/delayed sales data means the reorder decision itself is triggered too late even with fast supplier lead time', 'This synthesizes the earlier replenishment-program-lead-time and sell-through-pattern-analysis discussions into a single strategic dependency insight — test-and-repeat buying requires BOTH capabilities working together as a genuine SYSTEM: fast sell-through data visibility alone is useless if the resulting reorder still takes too long to arrive (missing the demand window), and fast supplier lead time alone is useless if the decision to actually PLACE that reorder is delayed by slow/lagging sales reporting — recognizing that this strategy depends on the WEAKEST LINK of these two capabilities (not either one in isolation) is a sophisticated, systems-level understanding of what actually makes test-and-repeat buying work in practice.', 3, '{"test-and-repeat-strategy-dependencies"}'::text[]),
('8b81a645-306d-4cc1-962a-ab597d640fe5', 'advanced', 'multiple_choice', 'A merchandiser evaluating supplier consolidation (reducing from 12 active suppliers down to 4 ''strategic'' suppliers, each receiving significantly larger order volumes) weighs this against the earlier-established order-allocation-diversification risk principle. Under what SPECIFIC condition would consolidation still be the WISER strategic choice DESPITE increasing single-supplier dependency risk?', '["Consolidation is never wiser under any circumstance, since the order-allocation-diversification principle always outweighs any possible benefit from supplier consolidation in every situation","When the CONSOLIDATION benefits (stronger volume-based negotiating leverage/better pricing, deeper technical/quality partnership investment, more consistent quality standards across a smaller supplier base, simplified vendor management overhead) meaningfully outweigh the incremental concentration risk — particularly when the remaining 4 suppliers are independently verified as highly reliable/financially stable (reducing the PROBABILITY of a disruption) and geographically/operationally diversified from EACH OTHER (reducing the chance of a single shared disruption event affecting multiple of the 4 simultaneously), making the residual concentration risk more manageable despite fewer total suppliers","Diversification risk considerations only apply to raw MATERIAL sourcing, never to finished GARMENT supplier relationships, making this entire tradeoff scenario technically inapplicable to supplier consolidation decisions","Consolidation is always automatically the wiser choice regardless of any risk consideration, since reducing supplier count is inherently and unconditionally beneficial in every scenario"]'::jsonb, 'When the CONSOLIDATION benefits (stronger volume-based negotiating leverage/better pricing, deeper technical/quality partnership investment, more consistent quality standards across a smaller supplier base, simplified vendor management overhead) meaningfully outweigh the incremental concentration risk — particularly when the remaining 4 suppliers are independently verified as highly reliable/financially stable (reducing the PROBABILITY of a disruption) and geographically/operationally diversified from EACH OTHER (reducing the chance of a single shared disruption event affecting multiple of the 4 simultaneously), making the residual concentration risk more manageable despite fewer total suppliers', 'This presents a genuine, nuanced resolution to the earlier order-allocation-diversification principle rather than treating it as an absolute rule — diversification is a risk-MANAGEMENT tool, not an end in itself, so a reasoned consolidation decision weighs the CONCRETE benefits (negotiating leverage, deeper quality/technical partnership, management simplicity) against the INCREMENTAL risk increase, and can still be the wiser choice when the remaining smaller supplier base is independently verified as reliable AND diversified from each other in ways that matter (different geographic regions/risk exposure, different financial backing) — reducing the PROBABILITY that the consolidated risk actually materializes, illustrating that sophisticated strategic sourcing decisions weigh risk MAGNITUDE and PROBABILITY together, not simply count the number of suppliers as a proxy for safety.', 3, '{"supplier-consolidation-risk-tradeoff"}'::text[]),
('8b81a645-306d-4cc1-962a-ab597d640fe5', 'advanced', 'multiple_choice', 'A merchandiser reviewing a season''s post-mortem finds a specific style had EXCELLENT sell-through (95%) but generated customer complaints/returns at a rate DOUBLE the company average, specifically citing ''runs small.'' The buying team argues this style was still a ''successful'' buy since it nearly sold out. Evaluate this claim using the earlier-established return-reason-analysis and forecast-accuracy-vs-in-season-management principles.', '["The buying team''s claim is fully correct with no qualification needed; 95% sell-through alone is a complete measure of buying success with zero need to consider return rate or customer complaint patterns","The claim is INCOMPLETE — high sell-through and high size-related returns can COEXIST, and are actually causally connected here: a ''runs small'' fit issue likely drove customers to buy MULTIPLE sizes to find one that fits (inflating apparent sell-through/demand) while the actual KEPT/final-sale rate is genuinely worse than the raw sell-through number suggests, meaning this style''s true performance (accounting for the return-driving fit defect) was likely worse than the buying team''s sell-through-only framing indicates — a classic case where a single metric (sell-through) taken alone provides an incomplete, potentially misleading performance picture","Sell-through rate and return rate are mathematically unrelated and cannot possibly be causally connected in any real buying scenario, making the buying team''s separate-issues framing the only valid interpretation","A ''runs small'' sizing complaint has no relationship to actual buying/merchandising performance and should be treated purely as a product development/technical design issue entirely disconnected from the buying team''s season performance evaluation"]'::jsonb, 'The claim is INCOMPLETE — high sell-through and high size-related returns can COEXIST, and are actually causally connected here: a ''runs small'' fit issue likely drove customers to buy MULTIPLE sizes to find one that fits (inflating apparent sell-through/demand) while the actual KEPT/final-sale rate is genuinely worse than the raw sell-through number suggests, meaning this style''s true performance (accounting for the return-driving fit defect) was likely worse than the buying team''s sell-through-only framing indicates — a classic case where a single metric (sell-through) taken alone provides an incomplete, potentially misleading performance picture', 'This synthesizes the earlier return-reason-analysis and sell-through-rate discussions into a genuinely sophisticated diagnostic insight — a ''runs small'' sizing problem doesn''t just cause returns in ISOLATION from sell-through; it can actually CAUSALLY INFLATE apparent sell-through, since customers uncertain about fit often buy multiple sizes intending to keep only one and return the rest, meaning the RAW sell-through number (95%) partly reflects this multi-size-purchasing behavior rather than genuine, clean demand for that quantity of product — the TRUE ''net kept'' performance (after accounting for the return-driving fit defect) is meaningfully worse than the headline sell-through figure suggests, exactly illustrating why a single metric evaluated in isolation (as the buying team is doing) can provide a misleadingly incomplete picture of a style''s actual performance.', 3, '{"sell-through-return-rate-interaction"}'::text[]);
